<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01911</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>be28cce2-9b41-4f5a-a8aa-9a9f17386fe1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_262d2f52-e31f-47b2-b66a-60f84a626b00" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A quadratic function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=(x-7)(x+3)\).</para>
            <para> Without graphing, identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph of \(f\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((7,0),(-3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((-7,0),(3,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,7),(0,-3)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,-7),(0,3)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((7,0)\) and \((-3,0)\). If \(x\) is \(7\), \((7-7)(7+3)\) gives (0,10), which is 0. If \(x\) is -3, \((-3-7)(-3+3)\) gives (-10,0), which is 0.</para>
            <para> Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.1 – 7.10.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((7,0)\0 and \((-3,0)\). If \(x\) is 7, \((7-7)(7+3)\) gives 0 10, which is 0. If \(x\) is -3, \((-3-7)(-3+3)\) gives -10 0, which is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f0164bac-d1a1-4b0c-b157-a5fc12ce0d62" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> Use the standard form of \((x-7)(x+3)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! – 21 The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is \((0,-21)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.1 – 7.10.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is – 21. The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is (0,-21).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_55ce37f4-0387-4fe9-b5ef-28c37e3645c7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>Answer these questions for the equation \(y=x^2-5x+4\).</para>
            <para> What is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of this equation’s graph?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 4 The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is (0,4).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 4. The \(y\)-intercept of the graph of the equation is \((0,4)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_68bfe074-e46a-4486-b055-66aa9b00bc06" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> An equivalent way to write this equation is \(y=(x-4)(x-1)\). What are the \(x\)-intercepts of this equation’s graph?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((1,0),(4,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((-1,0),(-4,0)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,1),(0,4)\)</para>
            <para>\((0,-1),(0,-4)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((1,0)\) and \((4,0)\) . If \(x\) is 1, \((1-4)(1-1)\) gives \((-3) (0)\) which is 0. If \(x\) is 4, \((4-4)(4-1)\) gives \((0) (3)\) which is 0.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((1,0)\) and \((4,0)\). If \(x\) is 1, \((1-4)(1-1)\) gives \((-3) (0)\) which is 0. If \(x\) is 4, \((4-4)(4-1)\) gives \((0) (3)\) which is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4d4d9630-91aa-4245-94f5-1a8c6d8fab42" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Noah said that if we graph \(y=(x-1)(x+6)\), the \(x\)-intercepts will be at \((1,0)\) and \((-6,0)\). Is Noah correct?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! We can check by substituting 1 for \(x\) and -6 for \(x\) and see if they produce an output of 0. If \(x\) is 1, \((1-1)(1+6)\) gives (0)(7), which is 0. If \(x\) is – 6, \((-6-1)(-6+6)\) gives (-7)(0), which is 0.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 – 7.10.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. We can check by substituting 1 for \(x\) and – 6 for \(x\) and see if they produce an output of 0. If \(x\) is 1, \((1-1)(1+6)\) gives (0)(7), which is 0. If \(x\) is – 6, \((-6-1)(-6+6)\) gives (-7)(0), which is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f272ae94-b434-4557-98ef-c4fcb2d3a5a9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> \(x\)-intercepts of this graph.
          
          <figure><media alt="A curve in an x y plane, origin O, with grid. Horizontal axis, scale negative 6 to 6, by 1’s. Vertical axis, scale negative 8 to 8, by 1’s. A curve passes through the points negative 1 comma 0, 1 comma negative 4, and 3 comma 0."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_12_10_01.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph crosses the \(x\)-axis at \((-1,0)\) and \((3,0)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.10.2 and 7.10.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is – 1 and 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3477c014-4164-47c2-a96d-60d6aefb0a20" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>Use the standard form of \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\) to identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para> Try again. Revisit activity 7.10.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 8.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
